                <pb facs="0897" n="890" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg890"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">603.002/401: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-28T23:00:00+09:00">November 28, 1937—11
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 28—12:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>577. Our 576, November 28, 4 p.m.</p>
                <p>1. In Craigie’s conversation with the Minister for Foreign Affairs today, Hirota
                    said that he would instruct the Japanese Consul General in Shanghai to give full
                    consideration to any plan proposed by Hall-Patch and gave assurances that no
                    decision would be taken until the latter’s views had been so considered.</p>
                <p>Report by mail Craigie is advising his Government to limit so far as possible
                    publicity regarding our concerted representations here because he fears that
                    such publicity would tend to make the Japanese military authorities in Shanghai
                    more intransigent. He realizes, however, that the present clamor in Parliament
                    may render publicity unavoidable.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
